<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-special-transformations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Special Transformations of <m>\R^2</m></title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Rotations, reflections, projections, and shears are the geometric transformations of
      the plane one meets most often. Each is linear, and so each is given by a
      <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix. Because a linear transformation preserves linear
      combinations, that matrix is completely determined by what the transformation does
      to the two standard basis vectors <m>\vec{e}_1</m> and <m>\vec{e}_2</m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-rotations">
    <title>Rotations in <m>\R^2</m></title>

    <definition xml:id="def-rotation">
      <title>Rotation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The transformation <m>R_\theta : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> denotes counterclockwise
          rotation about the origin through an angle of <m>\theta</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <remark xml:id="rem-rotation-is-linear">
      <title>Question: Is <m>R_\theta : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> a linear transformation?</title>
      <p>
        Yes. Rotating the whole plane rigidly about the origin carries the parallelogram
        determined by <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> to the parallelogram determined by
        <m>R_\theta(\vec{u})</m> and <m>R_\theta(\vec{v})</m>, so
        <m>R_\theta(\vec{u} + \vec{v}) = R_\theta(\vec{u}) + R_\theta(\vec{v})</m>;
        <m>R_\theta</m> preserves vector addition. Likewise, rotating a vector and then
        scaling it by <m>c</m> gives the same result as scaling first and then rotating,
        so <m>R_\theta(c\vec{u}) = c R_\theta(\vec{u})</m>; <m>R_\theta</m> preserves
        scalar multiplication.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <p>
      To find the matrix, we only need the images of <m>\vec{e}_1</m> and
      <m>\vec{e}_2</m>. Rotating <m>\vec{e}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>
      counterclockwise through <m>\theta</m> lands on the point at angle <m>\theta</m> on
      the unit circle, and rotating <m>\vec{e}_2</m> lands a further quarter turn along:
      <md>
        R_\theta(\vec{e}_1) = \begin{bmatrix} \cos\theta \\ \sin\theta \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        R_\theta(\vec{e}_2) = \begin{bmatrix} -\sin\theta \\ \cos\theta \end{bmatrix}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-rotation-basis">
      <caption>
        The images of the standard basis vectors under <m>R_\theta</m>. These two columns
        are exactly the columns of the rotation matrix.
      </caption>
      <image width="52%">
        <prefigure label="pf-rotation-basis" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(350,220)" margins="10">
            <coordinates bbox="(-1.7,-0.5,1.6,1.5)">
              <group at="rotation-basis">
                <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" arrows="2" decorations="no"/>
                <arc at="unit-circle" center="(0,0)" radius="1" range="(0,180)" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
                <group at="angle-e1">
                  <arc center="(0,0)" radius="0.34" range="(0,50)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                  <label p="(0.42,0.20)" alignment="e" offset="(0,0)"><m>\theta</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="angle-e2">
                  <arc center="(0,0)" radius="0.34" range="(90,140)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                  <label p="(-0.20,0.42)" alignment="n" offset="(0,0)"><m>\theta</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="vector-e1">
                  <line endpoints="((0,0),(1,0))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
                  <label p="(1,0)" alignment="se" offset="(0,-10)" color="#4A5D23"><m>\vec{e}_1</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="vector-e2">
                  <line endpoints="((0,0),(0,1))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
                  <label p="(0,1)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,4)" color="#4A5D23"><m>\vec{e}_2</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="image-e1">
                  <line endpoints="((0,0),(0.643,0.766))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
                  <label p="(0.643,0.766)" alignment="e" offset="(8,4)" color="#5E011C"><m>R_\theta(\vec{e}_1)</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="image-e2">
                  <line endpoints="((0,0),(-0.766,0.643))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
                  <label p="(-0.766,0.643)" alignment="w" offset="(-8,6)" color="#876801"><m>R_\theta(\vec{e}_2)</m></label>
                </group>
              </group>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="rotation-basis" text="The standard basis vectors and their images under the rotation R theta, drawn with the same scale on both axes">
                <annotation ref="unit-circle" text="The upper half of the unit circle; all four vectors end on it, since a rotation does not change length"/>
                <annotation ref="vector-e1" text="The olive vector e 1, from the origin to the point 1 comma 0 on the x axis"/>
                <annotation ref="vector-e2" text="The olive vector e 2, from the origin to the point 0 comma 1 on the y axis"/>
                <annotation ref="image-e1" text="The wine vector R theta of e 1, in the first quadrant, ending at the point cosine theta comma sine theta on the unit circle"/>
                <annotation ref="angle-e1" text="The angle theta, measured counterclockwise from e 1 to R theta of e 1"/>
                <annotation ref="image-e2" text="The mustard vector R theta of e 2, in the second quadrant, ending at the point minus sine theta comma cosine theta on the unit circle; it is perpendicular to R theta of e 1"/>
                <annotation ref="angle-e2" text="The same angle theta, measured counterclockwise from e 2 to R theta of e 2"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Therefore the matrix of <m>R_\theta</m> is
      <md>
        A_{R_\theta} = \begin{bmatrix}
          \cos\theta \amp -\sin\theta \\
          \sin\theta \amp \cos\theta
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-rotation-quarter-turn">
      <title>A Quarter Turn</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The transformation <m>R_{\pi/2} : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> denotes a counterclockwise
          rotation about the origin through an angle of <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m> radians. Find
          the matrix of <m>R_{\pi/2}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            A_{R_{\pi/2}} = \begin{bmatrix}
              \cos\frac{\pi}{2} \amp -\sin\frac{\pi}{2} \\
              \sin\frac{\pi}{2} \amp \cos\frac{\pi}{2}
            \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          so that
          <md>
            A_{R_{\pi/2}} \vec{x}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
              \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -y \\ x \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          For instance, with <m>\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> we get
          <m>R_{\pi/2}(\vec{x}) = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>. As expected
          for a quarter turn, the dot product is zero:
          <md>
            \vec{x} \cdot R_{\pi/2}(\vec{x}) = (2)(-1) + (1)(2) = 0.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <figure xml:id="fig-rotation-example">
      <caption>
        A quarter turn sends <m>\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> to
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>; the two vectors are perpendicular.
      </caption>
      <image width="52%">
        <prefigure label="pf-rotation-example" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(332,248)" margins="10">
            <coordinates bbox="(-2.4,-1.0,2.8,2.8)">
              <group at="quarter-turn">
                <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" arrows="2" hticks="(-2,1,2)" vticks="(-1,1,2)"/>
                <group at="right-angle">
                  <line endpoints="((0.268,0.134),(0.134,0.402))" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                  <line endpoints="((0.134,0.402),(-0.134,0.268))" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                </group>
                <group at="vector-x">
                  <line endpoints="((0,0),(2,1))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
                  <label p="(2,1)" alignment="e" offset="(6,4)" color="#4A5D23"><m>\vec{x}</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="image-x">
                  <line endpoints="((0,0),(-1,2))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
                  <label p="(-1,2)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,6)" color="#5E011C"><m>R_{\pi/2}(\vec{x})</m></label>
                </group>
              </group>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="quarter-turn" text="A vector x and its image under a quarter turn, drawn with the same scale on both axes">
                <annotation ref="vector-x" text="The olive vector x, from the origin to the point 2 comma 1"/>
                <annotation ref="image-x" text="The wine vector R pi over 2 of x, from the origin to the point minus 1 comma 2; it has the same length as x"/>
                <annotation ref="right-angle" text="A small square at the origin marks the right angle between x and its image"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="eg-half-turn-twice">
      <title>Twice Gives a Half Turn: Must It Be a Quarter Turn?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each nonzero real number <m>a</m>, let <m>T_a : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> be the
          transformation
          <md>
            T_a \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -ay \\ \frac{x}{a} \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Show that applying <m>T_a</m> twice sends every vector to its negative:
                <m>T_a(T_a(\vec{x})) = -\vec{x}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Does it follow that <m>T_a</m> is a quarter turn? Determine exactly which
                <m>a</m> make <m>T_a</m> a rotation through a quarter turn, and say which
                way it turns.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Give a concrete counterexample to the claim: <q>if applying a linear
                transformation twice gives a half turn, then the transformation is a
                quarter turn.</q>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(a)</term> Apply the rule twice:
          <md>
            T_a\!\left( T_a \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} \right)
            = T_a \begin{bmatrix} -ay \\ \frac{x}{a} \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -a \cdot \frac{x}{a} \\ \frac{-ay}{a} \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -x \\ -y \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          This holds for every <m>a \neq 0</m>. Sending each vector to its negative is the
          half turn <m>R_\pi</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(b)</term> No. <m>T_a</m> is the matrix transformation with matrix
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -a \\ \frac{1}{a} \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad \text{so} \qquad
            T_a(\vec{e}_1) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ \frac{1}{a} \end{bmatrix},
            \quad
            T_a(\vec{e}_2) = \begin{bmatrix} -a \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          A rotation does not change the length of a vector. Since <m>\vec{e}_1</m> and
          <m>\vec{e}_2</m> have length <m>1</m>, a rotation needs
          <m>\left| \frac{1}{a} \right| = 1</m> and <m>|a| = 1</m>, that is,
          <m>a = \pm 1</m>. For <m>a = 1</m> the matrix is
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, the counterclockwise
          quarter turn <m>R_{\pi/2}</m> of
          <xref ref="eg-rotation-quarter-turn" text="type-global" />. For <m>a = -1</m>
          it is <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, the matrix of
          <m>R_{-\pi/2}</m>, the clockwise quarter turn. For every other <m>a</m>,
          <m>T_a</m> is not a rotation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(c)</term> Take <m>a = 2</m>, so
          <m>T_2 \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} -2y \\ \frac{x}{2} \end{bmatrix}</m>. By part (a), applying
          <m>T_2</m> twice gives the half turn. But
          <m>T_2(\vec{e}_1) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ \frac{1}{2} \end{bmatrix}</m> has length
          <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>, so <m>T_2</m> is not a rotation. It does not even turn every
          vector through a right angle: for
          <m>\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <md>
            T_2(\vec{x}) = \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ \frac{1}{2} \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \vec{x} \cdot T_2(\vec{x}) = (1)(-2) + (1)\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\right)
            = -\tfrac{3}{2} \neq 0,
          </md>
          unlike the check at the end of
          <xref ref="eg-rotation-quarter-turn" text="type-global" />.
        </p>
        <p>
          The identity <m>T(T(\vec{x})) = -\vec{x}</m> is satisfied by many
          transformations. What singles out the quarter turns among them is geometric:
          a rotation also preserves lengths.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-reflections">
    <title>Reflections in <m>\R^2</m></title>

    <example xml:id="eg-reflection-x-axis">
      <title>Reflection in the <m>x</m>-Axis and in the Line <m>y = x</m></title>
      <p>
        Two reflections can be read directly from what they do to coordinates. In each
        case the columns of the matrix are <m>T(\vec{e}_1)</m> and <m>T(\vec{e}_2)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Reflection in the <m>x</m>-axis</term> transforms
              <m>\begin{bmatrix} a \\ b \end{bmatrix}</m> to
              <m>\begin{bmatrix} a \\ -b \end{bmatrix}</m>. Here
              <m>T(\vec{e}_1) = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
              <m>T(\vec{e}_2) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, so
              <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Reflection in the line <m>y = x</m></term> transforms
              <m>\begin{bmatrix} a \\ b \end{bmatrix}</m> to
              <m>\begin{bmatrix} b \\ a \end{bmatrix}</m>. Here
              <m>T(\vec{e}_1) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
              <m>T(\vec{e}_2) = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, so
              <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        Both are matrix transformations, <m>T_A(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> with
        <m>\vec{x} \in \R^2</m>.
      </p>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-reflection-y-mx">
      <title>Reflection in the Line <m>y = mx</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The reflection in the line <m>y = mx</m> is a linear transformation. Find the
          matrix corresponding to this transformation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Let us denote the reflection in the line <m>y = mx</m> by
          <m>Q_m : \R^2 \to \R^2</m>. We need to compute <m>Q_m(\vec{e}_1)</m> and
          <m>Q_m(\vec{e}_2)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let <m>\theta</m> be the angle the line <m>y = mx</m> makes with the positive
          <m>x</m>-axis. The point <m>(1, m)</m> lies on the line and is at distance
          <m>\sqrt{m^2+1}</m> from the origin, so
          <md>
            \cos\theta = \frac{1}{\sqrt{m^2+1}}, \qquad
            \sin\theta = \frac{m}{\sqrt{m^2+1}}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Reflecting <m>\vec{e}_1</m> in that line rotates it through <m>2\theta</m>, so
          <md>
            Q_m(\vec{e}_1)
            = \begin{bmatrix} \cos(2\theta) \\ \sin(2\theta) \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} \cos^2\theta - \sin^2\theta \\ 2\sin\theta\cos\theta \end{bmatrix}
            = \frac{1}{m^2+1} \begin{bmatrix} 1 - m^2 \\ 2m \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Exercise.</term> Show that
          <m>Q_m(\vec{e}_2) = \dfrac{1}{m^2+1} \begin{bmatrix} 2m \\ m^2-1 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          and hence that the matrix corresponding to <m>Q_m</m> is
          <md>
            \frac{1}{m^2+1} \begin{bmatrix}
              1 - m^2 \amp 2m \\
              2m \amp m^2 - 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <figure xml:id="fig-reflection-y-mx">
      <caption>
        Reflection in the line <m>y = mx</m>. The line makes an angle <m>\theta</m> with
        the <m>x</m>-axis, so <m>\vec{e}_1</m> is carried to the vector at angle
        <m>2\theta</m>.
      </caption>
      <image width="55%">
        <prefigure label="pf-reflection-y-mx" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(344,290)" margins="10">
            <coordinates bbox="(-1.7,-1.0,1.9,2.0)">
              <group at="reflection">
                <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" arrows="2" decorations="no"/>
                <group at="mirror-line">
                  <line endpoints="((-0.62,-0.93),(1.2,1.8))" stroke="#606060" thickness="2"/>
                  <label p="(1.15,1.75)" alignment="e" offset="(4,4)" color="#606060"><m>y=mx</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="angle-theta">
                  <arc center="(0,0)" radius="0.42" range="(0,56.31)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                  <label p="(0.47,0.17)" alignment="e" offset="(2,0)"><m>\theta</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="angle-two-theta">
                  <arc center="(0,0)" radius="0.64" range="(0,112.62)" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
                  <label p="(0.24,0.68)" alignment="n" offset="(0,4)"><m>2\theta</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="vector-e1">
                  <line endpoints="((0,0),(1,0))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
                  <label p="(1,0)" alignment="se" offset="(0,-10)" color="#4A5D23"><m>\vec{e}_1</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="image-e1">
                  <line endpoints="((0,0),(-0.385,0.923))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
                  <label p="(-0.385,0.923)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,6)" color="#5E011C"><m>Q_m(\vec{e}_1)</m></label>
                </group>
              </group>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="reflection" text="The vector e 1 and its mirror image in the line y equals m x, drawn with the same scale on both axes">
                <annotation ref="mirror-line" text="The grey line y equals m x through the origin, rising to the right; here it is steeper than the diagonal"/>
                <annotation ref="vector-e1" text="The olive vector e 1, from the origin to the point 1 comma 0 on the x axis"/>
                <annotation ref="angle-theta" text="The angle theta from the positive x axis up to the line y equals m x"/>
                <annotation ref="image-e1" text="The wine vector Q m of e 1, the mirror image of e 1 in the line; it has length 1 and lies as far beyond the line as e 1 lies before it"/>
                <annotation ref="angle-two-theta" text="The angle from the positive x axis to Q m of e 1 is 2 theta, so Q m of e 1 ends at the point cosine 2 theta comma sine 2 theta"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="eg-mirror-from-pair">
      <title>Finding the Mirror from One Vector and Its Image</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          An unknown reflection <m>Q</m> in a line through the origin sends
          <m>\vec{u} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> to
          <m>\vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Find one direction that <m>Q</m> fixes and one direction that <m>Q</m>
                reverses, and determine the mirror line.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the matrix of <m>Q</m> from those two directions, without the formula
                of <xref ref="eg-reflection-y-mx" text="type-global" />. Then compare with
                that formula.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Verify that <m>Q(\vec{u}) = \vec{v}</m>, and that
                <m>Q(Q(\vec{x})) = \vec{x}</m> for every <m>\vec{x}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(a)</term> Reflecting twice in the same line returns every vector to where
          it started, so <m>Q(\vec{v}) = Q(Q(\vec{u})) = \vec{u}</m>: the reflection
          interchanges <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m>. Since <m>Q</m> is linear,
          <md>
            Q(\vec{u} + \vec{v}) = \vec{v} + \vec{u},
            \qquad
            Q(\vec{u} - \vec{v}) = \vec{v} - \vec{u} = -(\vec{u} - \vec{v}).
          </md>
          So <m>Q</m> fixes
          <m>\vec{f} = \vec{u} + \vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
          reverses
          <m>\vec{g} = \vec{u} - \vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>. A
          reflection fixes exactly the vectors on its mirror, so the mirror is the line
          through <m>\vec{f}</m>, namely <m>y = 3x</m>. As a check, the reversed direction
          is perpendicular to the mirror:
          <m>\vec{f} \cdot \vec{g} = (1)(3) + (3)(-1) = 0</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(b)</term> Write a general vector as
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} = \alpha\vec{f} + \beta\vec{g}</m> and
          solve for <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\beta</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp x \\ 3 \amp -1 \amp y
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{-3r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp x \\ 0 \amp -10 \amp y - 3x
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-\frac{1}{10} r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp x \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp \frac{3x - y}{10}
              \end{array} \right]
              \xrightarrow{-3r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{x + 3y}{10} \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp \frac{3x - y}{10}
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          So <m>\alpha = \frac{x + 3y}{10}</m> and <m>\beta = \frac{3x - y}{10}</m>. By
          linearity, and because <m>Q(\vec{f}) = \vec{f}</m> and
          <m>Q(\vec{g}) = -\vec{g}</m>,
          <md>
            Q \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
            = \alpha\vec{f} - \beta\vec{g}
            = \frac{x + 3y}{10} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}
            - \frac{3x - y}{10} \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \frac{1}{5} \begin{bmatrix} -4x + 3y \\ 3x + 4y \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          and the matrix of <m>Q</m> is
          <m>\dfrac{1}{5} \begin{bmatrix} -4 \amp 3 \\ 3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>. The
          formula of <xref ref="eg-reflection-y-mx" text="type-global" /> with
          <m>m = 3</m> gives
          <m>\dfrac{1}{10} \begin{bmatrix} 1 - 9 \amp 6 \\ 6 \amp 9 - 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          the same matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(c)</term> First,
          <md>
            \frac{1}{5} \begin{bmatrix} -4 \amp 3 \\ 3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \frac{1}{5} \begin{bmatrix} -5 \\ 10 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Second, for <m>\vec{x} = \alpha\vec{f} + \beta\vec{g}</m>,
          <md>
            Q(Q(\vec{x})) = Q(\alpha\vec{f} - \beta\vec{g})
            = \alpha\vec{f} + \beta\vec{g} = \vec{x}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The directions <m>\vec{u} + \vec{v}</m> and <m>\vec{u} - \vec{v}</m> are the
          natural ones here: because <m>Q</m> interchanges <m>\vec{u}</m> and
          <m>\vec{v}</m>, their sum does not change and their difference changes sign.
          No slope had to be guessed.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-shear-projection">
    <title>Reflection through the Origin, Projections, and Shears</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-reflection-origin">
      <title>Reflection through the Origin</title>
      <p>
        Reflection through the origin sends
        <m>\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}</m> to
        <m>T(\vec{x}) = \begin{bmatrix} -x \\ -y \end{bmatrix}</m>, so
        <md>
          A = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
        This can also be thought of as <m>R_\pi</m>, since
        <md>
          R_\pi = \begin{bmatrix} \cos\pi \amp -\sin\pi \\ \sin\pi \amp \cos\pi \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-projection-x-axis">
      <title>Projection into the <m>x</m>-Axis</title>
      <p>
        Projection into the <m>x</m>-axis sends
        <m>\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}</m> to
        <m>T(\vec{x}) = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, flattening the whole
        plane onto the <m>x</m>-axis. Since
        <m>T(\vec{e}_1) = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
        <m>T(\vec{e}_2) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
        <md>
          A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Note the contrast with <xref ref="eg-reflection-x-axis" text="type-global" />: a
        reflection is one-to-one, while this projection is not, since every vector
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}</m> has the same image regardless of
        <m>y</m>.
      </p>
    </example>

    <figure xml:id="fig-projection-x-interactive">
      <caption>
        Projection into the <m>x</m>-axis,
        <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
        The deep wine vertical line on the left is the set of all inputs with the same image
        as <m>\vec{x}</m>; the olive green line on the right is the range, just the
        <m>b_1</m>-axis. Click and drag either vector.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="int-projection-x-interactive" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=1,0:0,0&amp;x=2,1.5&amp;show=true" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="eg-projection-from-directions">
      <title>A Projection Built from What It Fixes and What It Collapses</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>m</m> be a real number. Consider the linear transformations
          <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> that fix
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and send
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ m \end{bmatrix}</m> to zero:
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ m \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                For which <m>m</m> does such a transformation exist, and when is it
                unique?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                When it exists, find its matrix, its range, and the set of inputs it
                sends to <m>\vec{0}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Show that <m>T(T(\vec{x})) = T(\vec{x})</m> for every <m>\vec{x}</m>. Is
                <m>T</m> one-to-one? Is it onto?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(a)</term> If <m>m = 1</m>, the two requirements concern the same nonzero
          vector, which would have to be both fixed and sent to zero. No such <m>T</m>
          exists.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let <m>m \neq 1</m>. Write a general vector as
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
          = \alpha \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
          + \beta \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ m \end{bmatrix}</m> and solve for <m>\alpha</m> and
          <m>\beta</m>:
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp x \\ 1 \amp m \amp y
            \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow{-r_1 + r_2}
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp x \\ 0 \amp m - 1 \amp y - x
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
          Since <m>m - 1 \neq 0</m>, there is a pivot in every row and every column, so
          every vector has exactly one such expression:
          <md>
            \beta = \frac{y - x}{m - 1},
            \qquad
            \alpha = x - \beta = \frac{mx - y}{m - 1}.
          </md>
          Linearity then leaves no choice:
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
            = \alpha\, T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            + \beta\, T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ m \end{bmatrix}
            = \frac{mx - y}{m - 1} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          So there is at most one such <m>T</m>. There is exactly one, because this
          formula is a matrix transformation, hence linear, and it meets both
          requirements: <m>(x, y) = (1, 1)</m> gives <m>\alpha = 1</m>, and
          <m>(x, y) = (1, m)</m> gives <m>\alpha = 0</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(b)</term> Reading off the coefficients of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, the
          matrix is
          <md>
            A = \frac{1}{m - 1} \begin{bmatrix} m \amp -1 \\ m \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Every output is a multiple of
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and every such multiple is its own
          image, so the range is
          <m>\operatorname{Span}\left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>,
          the line <m>y = x</m>. The inputs sent to <m>\vec{0}</m> are those with
          <m>mx - y = 0</m>: the line <m>y = mx</m>, which is
          <m>\operatorname{Span}\left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ m \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(c)</term> For every <m>\vec{x}</m> the output is
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = \alpha \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and <m>T</m>
          fixes <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, so
          <md>
            T(T(\vec{x})) = \alpha\, T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \alpha \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = T(\vec{x}).
          </md>
          <m>T</m> is not one-to-one, since the nonzero vector
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ m \end{bmatrix}</m> is sent to <m>\vec{0}</m>
          (<xref ref="thm-injective-iff-trivial" text="type-global" />). It is not onto,
          since <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> is not a multiple of
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Applying <m>T</m> a second time changes nothing, which is the characteristic
          behaviour of a projection. This one projects the plane onto the
          line <m>y = x</m> along the direction
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ m \end{bmatrix}</m>. The projection of
          <xref ref="eg-projection-x-axis" text="type-global" /> is built the same way: it
          fixes <m>\vec{e}_1</m> and sends <m>\vec{e}_2</m> to zero. For <m>m = -1</m> the
          collapsed direction is perpendicular to the line <m>y = x</m>, and
          <m>A = \dfrac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-vertical-shear">
      <title>Vertical Shear</title>
      <p>
        A vertical shear <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> fixes <m>\vec{e}_2</m> and tilts
        <m>\vec{e}_1</m>:
        <md>
          T(\vec{e}_2) = \vec{e}_2, \qquad
          T(\vec{e}_1) = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ k \end{bmatrix},
        </md>
        so that
        <md>
          A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ k \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
        A shear leaves the vertical direction alone and slides each point vertically by an
        amount proportional to its <m>x</m>-coordinate, carrying a square to a
        parallelogram of the same area.
      </p>
    </example>

    <figure xml:id="fig-vertical-shear">
      <caption>
        A vertical shear with <m>k \lt 0</m> carries the unit square to a parallelogram.
      </caption>
      <image width="72%">
        <prefigure label="pf-vertical-shear" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(450,195)" margins="10">
            <coordinates bbox="(-1.2,-1.6,7.4,1.9)">
              <group at="vertical-shear">
                <polygon at="unit-square" points="((0,0),(1,0),(1,1),(0,1))" fill="#5E011C" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1"/>
                <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" arrows="2" decorations="no"/>
                <polygon at="parallelogram" points="((5,0),(6,-0.8),(6,0.2),(5,1))" fill="#5E011C" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1"/>
                <group at="image-e1">
                  <line endpoints="((5,0),(6,-0.8))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
                  <label p="(5.9,-0.9)" alignment="se" offset="(4,-4)" color="#4A5D23"><m>T(\vec{e}_1)</m></label>
                </group>
              </group>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="vertical-shear" text="A unit square on the left and, drawn further to the right for comparison, the parallelogram that a vertical shear with negative k makes of it">
                <annotation ref="unit-square" text="The unit square, with its lower left corner at the origin and sides along the two axes"/>
                <annotation ref="parallelogram" text="The image of the square: its left and right sides are still vertical and of length 1, but the right side has slid down, so the bottom and top edges slope down to the right"/>
                <annotation ref="image-e1" text="The olive vector T of e 1 runs along the bottom edge of the parallelogram: one unit to the right and k units up, which is down because k is negative"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-shear-interactive">
      <caption>
        A vertical shear with <m>k = 1</m>, so
        <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
        Drag <m>\vec{x}</m>: vertical vectors are fixed, everything else slides
        vertically. The solution set (deep wine) is always a single point and the range
        (olive green) is all of <m>\R^2</m>, so a shear is one-to-one and onto.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="int-shear-interactive" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=1,0:1,1&amp;x=2,1&amp;show=true" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
    </figure>
  </subsection>
</section>
